{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"25464faf-de35-487d-80c8-05bd17310fd6","agentId":"a00473bf11fbca24d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Diagnose the Baylor Football GPS import pipeline and design a regular-pull setup. This is investigation only.\n\nHARD RULES\n- Do NOT write to `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/gps_report/data/gps_history.duckdb`. If you need to test a write/import, copy the DB to `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/764640dd/tmp/gps-test/` and point the code at the copy (or open the original with `read_only=True`).\n- Do not commit, push, move, or delete anything. Do not create launchd/cron/scheduled tasks.\n- Never print the Catapult token or any secret. Load it from `.env` inside a script; never echo it.\n- Do not paste athlete-identifiable rows (names + metrics) in your report. Counts, dates, column names, activity names/IDs are fine.\n- Read-only GET calls to the Catapult OpenField API are allowed (e.g. list activities). Keep them few.\n- Machine traps: macOS, zsh. No `timeout` binary. Python is PEP 668 managed: use the project's existing venv if there is one, or `uv run`/`uv venv` in the job tmp dir — never global pip. A command guard (`dcg`) blocks shell `>` redirects into home paths, `mv` on home paths, `rm -rf`, and piping into `python3 -c`; write a small .py file in the job tmp dir and run it instead.\n\nCONTEXT\n- Project: `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/gps_report/` (git repo erash11/DataBase_GPS_Reporting). Files: run_report.py, bulk_import.py, backfill_velocity.py, compute_norms.py, config.yaml, src/ (api_client.py, storage.py, ...), logs/, Reports/, README.md, .env (CATAPULT_TOKEN).\n- DuckDB: newest session 2026-04-25, last good import 2026-04-30. Logs `logs/run_2026MMDD_2000*.log` from May to 2026-08-04 show only \"Bulk import started\" then nothing. No launchd job on this Mac mentions GPS; the scheduler probably lived on Eric's Windows laptop (maybe task `Refresh_All_Data`). Both machines are active; the DuckDB is gitignored so it does NOT sync between machines.\n- Manual API pulls worked on 2026-09-06/07 (JSON in `~/DEV/Catapult_API/`, 09-05 Auburn game).\n- Raw OpenField CSV exports for 2026 camp/season (08-25 → 09-04 practices, 09-05 game files) are in Google Drive `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/02_SPORTS/Football/FA2026/01_Raw_GPS/` (and `_INBOX`, `_Tools` sorting scripts there).\n- There is also a Printing Press CLI `catapult-openfield-connect-pp-cli` (skill `~/.claude/skills/pp-catapult-openfield-connect/SKILL.md`; store `~/.local/share/catapult-openfield-connect-pp-cli/`; binary may be in `~/go/bin` but not on PATH).\n- Readers of the DB: `coach_report/src/data.py` (find it — probably under ~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting or nearby) and `~/DEV/AgentAssist/tools/archive.py`.\n- Eric's constraint: local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Nothing that breaks mid-season. He wants regular pulls to keep reporting current.\n\nANSWER THESE\n1. How does the pipeline work end to end (run_report.py vs bulk_import.py: what API endpoints, what date window, how it decides what's new, what it writes, what reports it makes)? Is there any CSV import path?\n2. Root cause of the stall after \"Bulk import started\". Reproduce safely against a DB copy with a narrow window if feasible (e.g. just 2026-04-26 → 2026-05-10, or the list-activities step only). Is it a hang (no HTTP timeout?), an auth/API change, a rate limit, a crash with swallowed error, a stdout-buffer issue under a scheduler, a Windows-path issue? Give file:line evidence.\n3. Does the API list 2026 activities (camp 08-25+ and the 09-05 game)? How many activities between 2026-04-26 and today? Roughly how long would a backfill take?\n4. Can the Auburn JSON in `~/DEV/Catapult_API/` and the FA2026 CSVs be loaded with the existing code, or is the API backfill enough on its own (making the CSVs a backup only)? Compare fields briefly.\n5. Find what scheduled the 20:00 run: search the repo (and ~/DEV) for .ps1/.bat/.xml task definitions, README instructions, `Refresh_All_Data`. \n6. Design for \"regular pulls\" — give a concrete recommendation: which machine owns the DuckDB (only one can), cadence (e.g. nightly vs after each practice), manual one-command vs launchd on the Mac vs Task Scheduler on Windows (weighing the Baylor IT constraint), what the minimal code fixes are (e.g. HTTP timeouts, incremental window from max session date, a run receipt/log line on success and failure, lock so two runs can't collide), how failures become visible, and how reports refresh after a pull. List the exact files you'd change.\n\nReport under ~900 words, plain and concrete, with a short \"fix list\" (ordered) at the end and an estimate of effort per item.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"27896eb3-36a0-420f-865f-815b3e0511cf","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:05:57.460Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"764640dd-fa00-46b9-a4d9-f83074a06408","version":"2.1.270","gitBranch":"main"}
